{
  "slug": "slow-roasted-highland-beef",
  "title": "Slow-roasted Highland beef",
  "surface": "recipes",
  "collection": "recipes",
  "content": {
    "root": {
      "type": "root",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Why this cut, why this method",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Highland cattle grow slowly and carry their fat differently, and the shoulder rewards a low oven and patience more than it rewards technique. This is the method we cook at the farmhouse when there are people to feed and jobs to finish before dinner.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "claim",
              "claimId": "c2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The herd is raised on pasture at The Branch and finished on grass",
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "version": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", and the herd records are open if you want to check. ",
              "version": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "claim",
              "claimId": "c1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Grass-finishing lifts omega-3 precursors and antioxidant levels relative to grain-finishing",
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "version": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " — that is the sourced version. We will not pretend to breed-specific numbers nobody has measured.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Ingredients",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A 1.5 to 2 kg Highland shoulder roast. Two brown onions, quartered. A whole garlic bulb, halved across. Three carrots in thick chunks. A cup of beef stock. Olive oil, salt, pepper, and two sprigs of rosemary from wherever rosemary survives at your place.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Method",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Take the roast out of the fridge an hour before cooking so it comes to the oven at room temperature. Set the oven to 140°C fan-forced.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Salt the meat well on every side. Brown it hard in an ovenproof pot with a little oil — all sides, no shortcuts, this is where the flavour is made. Lift it out and set it aside.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Soften the onions and carrots in the same pot for five minutes, add the garlic cut-side down, then return the roast to sit on top. Pour in the stock, add the rosemary, and put the lid on.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Into the oven for three and a half to four hours. It is done when a fork twists through the middle without argument. If it argues, give it another half hour.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rest it for twenty minutes with the lid ajar before carving. The vegetables and pot juices are the sauce — mash the garlic into them and taste before you add anything.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "tag": "h2",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Where it comes from",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The paddocks behind this roast have a published baseline — the measuring is at ",
              "version": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "fields": {
                "url": "/measuring-soil-carbon-baseline",
                "linkType": "custom"
              },
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "the baseline post",
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "version": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", numbers and all. Dinner and the evidence, from the same ground.",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "version": 1,
      "format": "",
      "indent": 0,
      "direction": null
    }
  }
}
